winewayland: default the SC_MOVE decline ON -- 11.10-73 shipped the conversion without it

REGRESSION FIX. 11.10-73 defaulted three of the four owned-subsurface switches ON
(CAPTIONED, OWNED_ZORDER, SUBSURFACE_NO_DEMOTE) and left out the fourth --
NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE -- which is the one that makes a converted window movable.

WAYLAND_SysCommand claims SC_MOVE for every window with wayland data and then does nothing for a
non-toplevel, so a captioned owned window promoted to a subsurface cannot be moved or resized at
all. On the operator's machine Premiere's HOME SCREEN was unmovable, unresizable and a couple of
pixels too wide from 11.10-73 onward. The build tester has been running this switch by hand since
68, which is why their reports never showed it.

Operator-confirmed today, three states:
  CAPTIONED=0                     -> "sized right and movable and resizable"  (isolates the cause)
  CAPTIONED on, DECLINE=1         -> "home screen is fixed, docking still works"
  both at the new defaults        -> "home screen is fixed"

WHY IT WAS OFF: 11.10-64 bisected the half-white splash to this patch (63: 0.0% white, 64: 73.6%).
That cause was separately found and fixed afterwards in zzzzzzzzzzt (subsurface-expose), so the
gating outlived its reason. Re-checked before flipping: FIVE labelled launches, operator-confirmed
clean each time. That matters because the 64 fault fired on EVERY launch and only the paint latency
varied -- five clean launches is inconsistent with it being back, where one would prove nothing.

⛔ Do NOT re-verify the splash with splash-white-probe.py. It reports the densest white tile
ANYWHERE on the desktop and cannot distinguish the splash from the main window painting in during
load. It called this a 1-in-3 regression today; the operator identified the flagged tile as the main
frame at (0,200) and the verdict was withdrawn. Ask the operator -- it is faster and it is right.

Also in here, for the panel-deselect investigation (all default ON = unchanged behaviour):
  NEUTRON_DEACTIVATE_ON_LEAVE=0  suppresses deactivation entirely
  NEUTRON_DEACT_CANCELMODE=0     suppresses only the WM_CANCELMODE send
  NEUTRON_DEACT_FOREGROUND=0     suppresses only the hand-off of foreground to the desktop window
These are what isolated the deselect to a single line. NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE also now reports
itself through neutron_gate(), so a log shows what was in force.

And a fix to a diagnostic that lied: the wlkillfg trace printed "-> DEACTIVATING" BEFORE the gates
decided, so with a gate off it described something that never happened. It now says
"DEACTIVATING (intent; gates below decide what actually runs)".

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -415,10 +415,53 @@
  * ⛔ Every behaviour change in this driver must be gated. This one was not, which is why turning
  * off the feature it belongs to did not restore the old behaviour. Default OFF = 11.10-63's
  * behaviour exactly. */
+static BOOL neutron_deact_cancelmode(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_DEACT_CANCELMODE", TRUE);
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_deact_foreground(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_DEACT_FOREGROUND", TRUE);
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_gate_deactivate_on_leave(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_DEACTIVATE_ON_LEAVE", TRUE);
+    return on;
+}
+
 static BOOL neutron_syscommand_decline(void)
 {
     static int on = -1;
-    if (on < 0) { const char *e = getenv("NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE"); on = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    /* NEUTRON: DEFAULT ON since 2026-09-02 (11.10-75). This is the other half of making a
+     * captioned owned window usable, and 11.10-73 shipped the conversion WITHOUT it: three of the
+     * four switches the build tester had been running since 68 were defaulted on, and this one --
+     * the one that makes a converted window movable -- was left off. WAYLAND_SysCommand claims
+     * SC_MOVE for every window with wayland data and then does nothing for a non-toplevel, so the
+     * Premiere home screen became unmovable, unresizable and a couple of px too wide. Operator,
+     * 2026-09-02: with CAPTIONED=0 it was "sized right and movable and resizable"; with
+     * CAPTIONED on and this switch on, "home screen is fixed, docking still works".
+     *
+     * It was gated off because 11.10-64 bisected the half-white splash to it (63: 0.0% white,
+     * 64: 73.6%). That cause was separately found and fixed afterwards in zzzzzzzzzzt (the
+     * subsurface-expose patch), so the gating outlived its reason. Re-checked before flipping:
+     * FIVE labelled launches, operator-confirmed clean each time. That matters because the 64
+     * fault fired on EVERY launch and only the paint latency varied -- so five clean launches is
+     * inconsistent with it being back, where one would prove nothing.
+     *
+     * ⛔ Do NOT re-verify this with splash-white-probe.py: it reports the densest white tile
+     * ANYWHERE on the desktop and cannot tell the splash from the main window painting in during
+     * load. It called this a 1-in-3 regression on 2026-09-02; the operator identified the tile as
+     * the main frame at (0,200) and the verdict was withdrawn. Ask the operator.
+     *
+     * Kill switch: NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE=0. */
+    if (on < 0) on = neutron_gate("NEUTRON_SYSCOMMAND_DECLINE", TRUE);
     return on;
 }
 
@@ -1527,17 +1570,37 @@
         ERR("neutron-wlkillfg: hwnd=%p focused=%p fg=%p style=%08x -> %s\n", hwnd, focused,
             NtUserGetForegroundWindow(), (unsigned)NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE),
             focused ? "SKIP (in-app focus change)" :
-            hwnd != NtUserGetForegroundWindow() ? "SKIP (not the foreground window)" : "DEACTIVATING");
+            hwnd != NtUserGetForegroundWindow() ? "SKIP (not the foreground window)" :
+            "DEACTIVATING (intent; gates below decide what actually runs)");
         if (focused) return 0;
 
+        /* NEUTRON: gate the deactivation itself, so "does losing foreground break the next click"
+         * can be answered without a rebuild. The operator reports a panel's selection border
+         * highlighting and then dropping, but ONLY after clicking away and back -- and the enter
+         * path is already ruled out by experiment (NEUTRON_KBD_FOREGROUND=0 changed nothing). This
+         * handler is what remains in that transition. Default ON = current behaviour; =0 restores
+         * the pre-zzzzzzzzzk state where Wine never told the app it had lost focus at all.
+         * ⚠️ =0 is a DIAGNOSTIC, not a fix: without it an application never learns it is no longer
+         * foreground, which is what this patch exists to correct. */
+        if (!neutron_gate_deactivate_on_leave())
+        {
+            ERR("neutron-wlkillfg: hwnd=%p DEACTIVATION SUPPRESSED (probe: "
+                "NEUTRON_DEACTIVATE_ON_LEAVE=0)\n", hwnd);
+            return 0;
+        }
+
         /* Messages sent below may themselves change the foreground window, so re-check rather
          * than trusting the state we saw when the leave arrived. */
         if (hwnd != NtUserGetForegroundWindow()) return 0;
 
-        if (!(NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE) & WS_MINIMIZE))
+        /* NEUTRON: the two halves of deactivation, separately gated. The operator's panel-deselect
+         * reproduces only after a refocus and is caused by this handler (proven: suppressing it
+         * entirely stops the drop), but "this handler" is two different actions and they fail
+         * differently. Split so one launch each says which. */
+        if (!(NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE) & WS_MINIMIZE) && neutron_deact_cancelmode())
             send_message(hwnd, WM_CANCELMODE, 0, 0);
 
-        if (hwnd == NtUserGetForegroundWindow())
+        if (hwnd == NtUserGetForegroundWindow() && neutron_deact_foreground())
         {
             TRACE("lost focus to another client, handing foreground to the desktop\n");
             NtUserSetForegroundWindowInternal(NtUserGetDesktopWindow());
